\section{Put the Cost Calculation in the Transition}

In ``Faster Pattern Selection with Offline Cost Analysis,'' Blindell distinguishes a shape matchset from a state that contains winning rules and relative costs \cite[section~3.6.3]{blindell2016instruction}. For an eager generated selector, a transition table maps the current opcode and ordered child state IDs to a new state ID. A separate rule table maps that state and a requested goal to a single winning rule. The runtime labels nodes bottom-up, then follows the recovered frontier goals from the root. The recurrence from Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-267} still determines the winners, but its candidate comparisons take place when a transition is generated, not at every node it labels.

The normalization argument is easiest for a \emph{linear-form} grammar in Blindell's sense: every base rule consumes one terminal and requires one goal from each of its immediate children. If a nested pattern is factored into such rules, the synthetic goals must preserve its operand bindings; the auxiliary rules do not emit additional machine instructions. For a child with finite costs, subtract its minimum finite cost $b$ from each finite entry, leaving unreachable entries infinite. Every viable base rule at the parent uses one contribution from that child, so removing $b$ subtracts the same amount from all those candidates. Same-node chain closure on the resulting costs, if needed, preserves that common shift. An all-infinite vector instead has a distinguished dead state; there is no finite offset to subtract.

\section{Generate a Small Closure}

Consider two register banks $W$ and $X$, both storing the same word bits. Argument values arrive in $W$. $\mathrm{inv}$ computes bitwise complement. The costs model several complement instructions with different source and destination banks.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{clrr}
Rule & Production & Weight \\
$a$ & $W\gets\mathrm{arg}$ & 0 \\
$b$ & $X\gets\mathrm{arg}$ & 3 \\
$c$ & $W\gets\mathrm{inv}(W)$ & 2 \\
$d$ & $W\gets\mathrm{inv}(X)$ & 5 \\
$e$ & $X\gets\mathrm{inv}(W)$ & 4 \\
$f$ & $X\gets\mathrm{inv}(X)$ & 3
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

In goal order $(W,X)$ the argument gives state $q_0$ with deltas $(0,3)$ and rules $(a,b)$. Applying $\mathrm{inv}$ to that state gives costs $(\min(2,8),\min(4,6))=(2,4)$, hence state $q_1$ with deltas $(0,2)$ and rules $(c,e)$. Applying $\mathrm{inv}$ again to $q_1$ gives $(\min(2,7),\min(4,5))=(2,4)$ in the child's coordinate system. The transition leads back to $q_1$. Its normalized result does not change with nesting depth.

This is a two-state instance of the work queue described in Blindell's discussion of Proebsting and BURG: seed from nullary symbols, combine queued states with known states for the operators' child positions, and enqueue only a new state. Any resulting fixpoint contains every reachable state, by induction on tree height. Store the reachability mask, deltas, and rule IDs as the state key with a deterministic tie policy. Rule IDs matter even when deltas agree: reduction needs the actual winning rule, not just its price. For this grammar and cost model, equal state keys are interchangeable in every parent transition.

