\section{Fill the Same Table on Demand}

Blindell's ``Generating States Lazily'' describes Ertl and colleagues' alternative: compute transitions as they are needed and reuse the resulting states \cite[section~3.6.4]{blindell2016instruction}. Here is the trace for three successive complements of one argument, starting with an empty cache.

\begin{verbatim}
occurrence     transition key     result    absolute (W,X)
arg            (arg, ())          q0 miss    (0,3)
inv(arg)       (inv, q0)          q1 miss    (2,4)  [1]
inv(inv(arg))  (inv, q1)          q1 miss    (4,6)  [2]
third inv      (inv, q1)          q1 hit     (6,8)

On a miss:
  compute candidates from child deltas and local weights
  remember winning rule per goal
  normalize; intern the result; memoize the transition
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{[1]} Interning $q_1$ stores a state shared by complements at several depths. It does not store $2$ as the absolute cost of implementing those complements in $W$. If absolute totals are useful for diagnostics, store an offset at each occurrence: sum the children's offsets and the minimum finite cost computed by this transition.

\noindent\textbf{[2]} A known result state is not a known transition. The key $(\mathrm{inv},q_1)$ still misses on its first appearance. The next complement hits that key; for final goal $X$ its rule is $e$, whose child needs $W$, regardless of the absolute cost at that child.

\section{When to Keep the Explicit Cost Pass}

\Cref{fig:visual-268} separates the transition keys from the costs of their individual occurrences. Three complements use just two distinct \texttt{inv} keys.

% Explanatory visual
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[tome diagram]
  \node[tome box,minimum width=2.9cm] (q0) at (0,0) {\(q_0:(0,3)\)\\Winners \(a,b\)};
  \node[tome box,minimum width=2.9cm] (q1) at (6,0) {\(q_1:(0,2)\)\\Winners \(c,e\)};
  \draw[tome arrow] (q0)--node[above,font=\footnotesize]{\(\mathrm{inv}\)} (q1);
  \draw[tome arrow] (q1.north east) to[out=55,in=125,looseness=3]
    node[above,font=\footnotesize]{\(\mathrm{inv}\)} (q1.north west);
  \foreach \x/\seq/\cost/\stat in {-0.9/{arg}/{(0,3)}/{seed miss},1.85/{first inv}/{(2,4)}/{new key},4.6/{second inv}/{(4,6)}/{new key},7.35/{third inv}/{(6,8)}/{cache hit}} {
    \node[align=center,font=\footnotesize] at (\x,-1.75) {\seq\\\(\cost\)\\\stat};
  }
  \draw[tome arrow] (-1.65,-1.11)--(8.1,-1.11);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The normalized automaton loops at \(q_1\), even as the absolute costs \((W,X)\) increase. The second complement finds an existing state but still adds a transition to a lazy cache.}
\label{fig:visual-268}
\end{figure}

Normalization preserves choices; it does not make every grammar finite-state. Change the example so both goals cost zero at the leaf, no cross-bank complement is legal, and each complement adds one in $W$ and two in $X$. At depth $n$ the normalized vector is $(0,n)$. For a grammar exposing both goals, eager enumeration of these exact vectors cannot terminate. A lazy cache only visits observed vectors but may still grow without useful reuse.

Costs that change with context also need care. If $f$ costs one rather than three under a different target mode, the same child $q_1$ now makes $f$ beat $e$ for $X$. Include such context in the cache key, or recompute the affected transitions. An explicit per-node cost pass is both a useful reference for differential tests and a sensible fallback when a cache would exceed its budget.
